.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt LUA-HALFBSD 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm lua-halfbsd
.Nd native Lua process and operating system interfaces
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The HalfBSD userland interpreter uses Lua 5.4.8 from
.Pa contrib/lua/src ,
the same source used by the boot loader and flua.
The boot loader uses a restricted freestanding environment;
this interpreter uses all ordinary userland standard libraries.
The installed static libraries are
.Pa liblua.a , libhalfproc.a ,
and
.Pa libhalfbsd.a .
The Lua C API headers are in
.Pa /usr/include/lua .
Applications call
.Fn halfbsd_openlibs
instead of
.Fn luaL_openlibs
to preload the native modules.
.Pp
This is a foundation for future orchestration.
Installing it does not modify init, rc, services, or shutdown behavior.
.Sh ERRORS AND OWNERSHIP
Invalid arguments raise Lua errors.
Operating system failures return
.Dq nil, error .
Error tables contain
.Sy kind , code , name , message , operation
and, when relevant,
.Sy stage .
The code is the native errno; an unmapped errno name is
.Sq UNKNOWN .
Timeouts have kind
.Sq timeout .
Lua allocation failures raise exceptions.
Nonzero child exits and signal termination are successful observations,
not execution failures; inspect the result table.
.Pp
A process result has
.Sy pid , state , own_group , started
and, after completion,
.Sy finished .
Times use the monotonic clock.
States are
.Sq running , exited , signaled ,
or
.Sq lost .
Exited results include
.Sy exit_code
and
.Sy success ;
signaled results include
.Sy signal , core_dumped
and false
.Sy success .
Lost results report native
.Sy code
and must not be treated as successful exits.
.Pp
Process descriptors identify children without PID reuse races.
A separate native thread owns reaping; Lua garbage collection does not
terminate children or abandon them as zombies.
Dropping the last Lua process handle does not stop a service.
Closing the Lua state lets its reaper continue until remaining children exit.
Explicitly terminate or shut down services before destroying their manager.
This library manages its own direct children, not arbitrary adopted children.
Do not install a competing waitpid reaper for these children.
.Pp
File descriptor handles have
.Sy close , fileno , nonblock , read
and
.Sy write
methods, and support Lua to-be-closed variables.
Close is idempotent.
Fileno returns the live descriptor number.
Nonblock takes a boolean and changes the shared open-file description.
Read takes an optional byte count, 1 through 1048576, default 65536.
It returns a string, nil at EOF, or nil and an error on failure.
Write takes a binary string of at most 64 MiB and returns bytes written;
partial writes are possible.
Nonblocking IO may return EAGAIN.
SIGPIPE from a broken pipe is reported as EPIPE without killing the caller.
.Pp
Process handles retain redirected IO resources until exit.
Pipe endpoints share resource ownership so accidental garbage collection does
not unexpectedly close the other endpoint.
Explicitly close unused pipe ends, particularly stdin writers, to deliver EOF.
Event registrations retain their own duplicated descriptors and targets;
explicitly cancel their watches before closing a watched descriptor.
.Sh PROCESS MODULE
.Bd -literal -offset indent
local proc = require("proc")
local child, err = proc.spawn({"/usr/sbin/example", "-f"}, {
    cwd="/var/empty", user="nobody", group="nogroup",
    inherit_env=false, env={PATH="/bin:/usr/bin"},
    stdin="null", stdout={path="/var/log/example",append=true},
    stderr="inherit", new_session=true,
})
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy spawn(argv, options)
requires a dense array of strings including the executable at index 1.
Executable paths are passed directly to execve; there is no shell or PATH search.
The call returns a handle only after a close-on-exec pipe confirms execution.
Failures identify their initialization stage.
A handle denotes creation, not application readiness.
The arguments and environment together must fit ARG_MAX.
At most 4096 arguments and 1024 active children per context are allowed.
.Pp
Options are:
.Bl -tag -width inherit_fds
.It Sy cwd
Working directory, changed before credential transition.
.It Sy env, inherit_env
Environment map of string names to string values or false to unset a name.
Inheritance defaults to true; false replaces the inherited environment entirely.
.It Sy user, group, groups
User and primary group names or numeric IDs.
A user must resolve in the account database so its supplementary groups can
be resolved before forking.
The optional dense numeric groups array replaces supplementary membership.
A group-only transition clears inherited supplementary groups.
Changes occur as setgroups, setresgid, then setresuid and fail closed.
.It Sy stdin, stdout, stderr
Default inherit, or the strings inherit, null, closed, or pipe;
a descriptor handle; or a table with path, append and mode.
File mode defaults to 0600. Symlink destinations are rejected.
Path redirections accept regular files and character devices; use descriptor
handles or anonymous pipes for other IO.
Automatic pipes are available as child.stdin, child.stdout and child.stderr.
They expose the parent-facing endpoint.
.It Sy new_session, process_group
Create a session, or a new process group without a new session.
New sessions also create a process group.
.It Sy inherit_fds
At most 256 explicit handles or descriptor numbers at least 3.
All other descriptors beyond the standard streams are closed in the child.
.It Sy exec_timeout
Bounded initialization/exec handshake, default 10 seconds.
.El
.Pp
.Sy run(argv, options)
returns a completed result table and accepts the same options plus:
.Bl -tag -width output_limit
.It Sy timeout
Optional nonnegative wall duration in seconds after execution was confirmed.
Timeouts terminate the child and return a timeout error.
.It Sy grace
TERM grace period before KILL, default 5 seconds.
Forced termination has another bounded 5-second reap deadline.
.It Sy input
A binary string, at most 64 MiB, automatically piped to stdin.
Automatic stdin pipes without input are closed to deliver EOF.
.It Sy output_limit
Per-stream captured output bound, default 1 MiB, maximum 64 MiB.
With stdout or stderr set to pipe, run drains both concurrently and places
captured strings in the result. Exceeding the bound terminates the child and
returns EFBIG rather than allocating without limit.
.El
.Pp
.Sy wait(child, timeout)
returns a completed result or a timeout error.
A missing timeout waits indefinitely; zero is nonblocking.
.Sy wait_any(children, timeout)
returns the dense array index and completed result.
It does not consume an already-observed result; remove completed handles from
the array before waiting for a different child.
.Sy status(child)
returns a current result without blocking.
.Sy signal(child, signal, group)
delivers a number or uppercase signal name, optionally prefixed SIG.
It returns true on delivery, false for an already-exited child, or an error.
.Sy kill(child, group)
is signal KILL.
.Sy terminate(child, grace, group)
sends TERM, waits, then KILL if needed and returns a completed result.
These functions are also child methods.
.Pp
Group signaling is permitted only for a session/group created by spawn and
only while its leader remains unreaped; it never signals a recycled group ID.
Descendants which escape the group require future job policy.
.Sy active()
returns a snapshot of active handles belonging to this context.
.Sy shutdown(grace, group)
terminates the snapshot with a shared grace deadline, followed by a shared
5-second forced deadline; group defaults to true for owned groups.
It returns true or an error.
.Sy pipe(nonblocking)
returns read and write handles.
.Sy fd(number)
duplicates an existing descriptor with close-on-exec protection.
.Sh EVENT MODULE
.Bd -literal -offset indent
local event = require("halfbsd.event")
local loop <close> = assert(event.new())
loop:watch_process(child, function(e)
    print(e.result.state)
    loop:stop()
end)
assert(loop:run())
.Ed
.Pp
New returns a loop or an error.
Every watch function returns a unique numeric cancellation ID or an error.
Each loop permits at most 4096 simultaneous watches.
Callbacks receive a table with id, count, flags and eof.
Descriptor and process events also carry target; process events carry result.
Callbacks run synchronously on the Lua thread, never in a signal handler.
.Bl -tag -width watch_process
.It Sy watch_process(child, callback)
One-shot process exit via EVFILT_PROCDESC, including registration after exit.
The native reaper still must perform pdwait; an exit notification alone is
not reaping. The callback receives the completed result.
.It Sy watch_timer(seconds, callback, repeating)
Positive interval, rounded upward to milliseconds.
Defaults to one shot. Kernel timers do not busy-wait.
.It Sy watch_signal(signal, callback)
EVFILT_SIGNAL with the prior disposition restored on cancellation.
A signal has one watcher process-wide. KILL, STOP and CHLD are not supported;
use process watches for managed child exits.
.It Sy watch_read(fd, callback), watch_write(fd, callback)
EV_CLEAR readiness. Use nonblocking handles and drain or fill until EAGAIN.
.It Sy watch_path(path, callback)
Observe vnode write, delete, rename, attribute and revoke changes.
Symlinks are rejected. Replacing a pathname does not retarget its old vnode;
cancel and watch again when following a replacement is necessary.
.It Sy cancel(id)
Returns true if canceled, false for a nonexistent ID.
.It Sy step(timeout)
Waits for at most one callback, returning true for delivery and false for timeout
or no watches. Timeout defaults to infinite and zero polls once.
.It Sy run()
Dispatches until stopped or no watches remain, returning true.
.It Sy stop()
Stops run after the current callback, preserving watches.
.It Sy close()
Cancels watches, restores signal dispositions and closes the kqueue.
Idempotent; the loop is unusable afterward.
.El
.Pp
Step and run return nil and an error on kernel failure.
A callback exception is returned with kind callback and its original cause.
Persistent watches remain registered; cancel or close before retrying.
Blocking callbacks delay all other callbacks. Loops are not thread-safe.
Dispatching the same loop recursively from a callback is rejected.
.Sh OPERATING SYSTEM MODULE
.Sy require("halfbsd.os")
provides the following functions.
Filesystem mutators return true or nil and an error.
.Bl -tag -width atomic_replace
.It Sy exists(path)
True or false; unexpected errors remain errors.
.It Sy stat(path, follow)
Metadata table: type, uid, gid, mode, size, inode, device, links, mtime.
Defaults to lstat; true follows the final link.
.It Sy mkdir(path, mode), rmdir(path), unlink(path)
Single-directory creation (default 0755) and explicit removal.
No recursive deletion.
.It Sy chmod(path, mode), chown(path, uid, gid)
Change final-entry metadata without following the final symlink.
Parent pathname components use ordinary filesystem semantics.
.It Sy symlink(target, path), readlink(path)
Create or read a link. Readlink returns a string or an error.
.It Sy open(path, mode, permissions)
Returns a descriptor; mode is read, write, append or exclusive.
Permissions default to 0600. Final symlinks are rejected.
.It Sy atomic_replace(path, data, mode)
Binary data up to 64 MiB; default 0600.
Walks parent components without following symlinks and rejects dot and dot-dot.
Creates an exclusive random temporary file in the same directory, writes,
sets permissions, fsyncs, renames and fsyncs the parent directory.
An error includes committed=true if rename succeeded but directory sync failed.
Replacement changes the file identity and ownership to the caller; it does not
preserve ACLs, extended attributes or the replaced inode's other metadata.
.It Sy hostname(), set_hostname(name)
Read the hostname string or explicitly change it.
.It Sy clock(kind)
Seconds from monotonic (default) or realtime clock.
.It Sy set_clock(seconds, nanoseconds)
Explicit privileged realtime clock_settime; integer seconds, optional
nanoseconds in 0 through 999999999.
.It Sy user(name_or_id), group(name_or_id)
Account metadata tables; failures preserve lookup errno.
User fields: name, uid, gid, home, shell. Group fields: name, gid, members.
.It Sy identity()
PID, PPID, UID/EUID, GID/EGID, process group, session and supplementary groups.
.It Sy getenv(name), setenv(name, value), environment()
Read a variable (nil if absent), set/unset it (nil unsets), or snapshot all.
.It Sy signal_disposition(signal, disposition)
Explicit ignore or default; rejects watched signals and KILL/STOP/CHLD.
Signal handlers which execute Lua asynchronously are deliberately absent.
.It Sy mount(options, flags), unmount(path, flags)
Explicit native nmount key/value strings (64 entries, at most 1 MiB) and unmount.
Flags are numeric and default to zero. These require appropriate privilege.
No filesystem is inferred and no shell is invoked.
.El
.Sh BOOT INVENTORY AND TERMINAL INTERFACES
The OS module provides the following explicit interfaces. They return a value,
or nil and a structured native error. FreeBSD inventories are bounded snapshots;
concurrent kernel changes may return EAGAIN and require a fresh query.
.Bl -tag -width interface_up
.It Sy fstab()
Array of source, path, type, options and pass fields. The libc cursor closes
before Lua table construction. At most 1024 entries and 1 MiB of strings.
.It Sy mounts()
Kernel mount records with source, path, type and readonly, using MNT_NOWAIT.
.It Sy processes()
Visible process records with pid, uid (effective), command and jail fields.
Names are kernel command names, not trustworthy executable identity proofs.
.It Sy default_routes()
IPv4 default routes with interface and gateway. It does not change routes.
.It Sy interface_up(name)
Explicit privileged ioctl setting IFF_UP while preserving other flags.
.It Sy terminal()
Table with tty, foreground and stop_background_output (TOSTOP) booleans for
standard input.
.It Sy terminal_restore()
Return the current terminal to this process's own living group.
.El
.Pp
FreeBSD link rows from
.Sy interfaces()
include link_type and available link_state fields.
.Sy child:foreground()
transfers standard-input terminal foreground ownership to a live tracked child
that owns its process group in the same session, then delivers SIGCONT through
its process descriptor. Reaping is locked out during the handoff. Reaped handles
return ESRCH; absent group ownership returns EINVAL. These operations do not
create a login, authenticate a user, or establish a PAM session.
.Pp
The IPC module adds
.Sy udp(numeric_ipv4, port)
for a connected nonblocking datagram descriptor and
.Sy tcp(numeric_ipv4, port)
for a managed nonblocking stream descriptor. TCP returns a descriptor even while
its connect is pending; retain it until connection completion or cancellation.
Both constructors validate ports 1..65535 and use close-on-exec descriptors.
.Sy connected(descriptor)
returns true after connection completion, false while pending, or nil and a
native error on failure. It polls without blocking and never closes the supplied
descriptor. After an error, close it: SO_ERROR retrieval consumes the socket error.
The caller must close successful, failed and cancelled connections explicitly.
Descriptors follow the existing read/write, nonblock and close contracts.
No name lookup or shell is invoked.
.Sh LOGGING MODULE
.Sy require("halfbsd.log").new(options)
returns a logger or an error.
Options: console (default true, stderr), file (optional append destination),
and service (default halfbsd).
The file must be regular, is close-on-exec, rejects a final symlink,
and is created mode 0600.
.Sy logger:write(level, message, fields)
returns true or nil and an error.
Levels: debug, info, notice, warning, error, critical.
Messages are binary strings up to 64 KiB; fields are at most 64 scalar values.
Records contain an epoch timestamp, severity, service, message and optional fields.
Control and non-ASCII bytes are escaped in JSON; no syslog daemon is required.
Numeric formatting is independent of the caller's numeric locale.
.Sy logger:close()
closes the optional file and is idempotent.
Console-only logging works before writable filesystems are available.
Failures to one output do not prevent an attempt to write the other output.
.Ss Boot filesystem operations
.Sy halfbsd.os.sync()
returns true after sync(2); it does not report per-filesystem durability errors.
.Sy halfbsd.os.swap_on(path)
and
.Sy halfbsd.os.swap_off(path)
wrap FreeBSD swapon(2) and swapoff(2) with zero flags.
They return true or nil and the structured native errno error.
These privileged operations do not create devices or files and do not infer
ownership; callers must track which swap devices they actually activated.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
These interfaces inherit the caller's authority and are not a sandbox.
Use trusted scripts and explicit credentials, environments and descriptors.
Privileged mount, hostname and metadata operations remain explicit.
Lua standard libraries still include shell execution interfaces; proc never
uses them implicitly.
No Capsicum sandbox is provided. Boot integration remains an explicit opt-in.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr lua 1 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr pdwait 2
.Sh EXAMPLES
Installed examples are in
.Pa /usr/share/examples/halfbsd .
